1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,464
This is a walkthrough of the payment reconciliation engine, recorded live against the full Docker Compose stack.

2
00:00:06,504 --> 00:00:14,245
For every payment it compares three record sets: the merchant's ledger, the payment gateway's settlement file, and the bank statement.

3
00:00:14,285 --> 00:00:21,097
It finds every mismatch, explains the cause, fixes the safe cases automatically, and keeps track of the money at risk.

4
00:00:21,667 --> 00:00:22,653
Security first.

5
00:00:22,693 --> 00:00:29,764
The API issues JSON web tokens, and every endpoint checks the user's role: analyst, supervisor or admin.

6
00:00:29,804 --> 00:00:36,054
A wrong password is rejected with the same structured error format that every endpoint uses.

7
00:00:36,094 --> 00:00:38,174
Now let's sign in as the admin.

8
00:00:39,718 --> 00:00:45,259
This is the failure simulator: a fake payment gateway and a fake bank that misbehave on purpose.

9
00:00:45,299 --> 00:00:56,710
From one seed it generates payments over any number of days and injects eleven kinds of fault, such as lost callbacks, duplicate webhooks, late settlements, fee differences and orphan bank credits.

10
00:00:56,750 --> 00:01:01,361
I'll raise the volume to three thousand payments a day and bump two fault rates.

11
00:01:01,853 --> 00:01:11,491
When it runs, the gateway publishes webhook callbacks to Kafka, including duplicates and out-of-order events, and writes a settlement file per day to S3 on LocalStack.

12
00:01:11,531 --> 00:01:17,054
The bank simulator regenerates the same world from the same seed and writes the bank statements.

13
00:01:17,094 --> 00:01:20,879
A ground-truth file records exactly which payment got which fault.

14
00:01:21,371 --> 00:01:22,391
Now we reconcile.

15
00:01:22,431 --> 00:01:34,178
The engine waits for the Kafka ledger consumer to catch up, ingests the settlement files and statements idempotently, then reconciles every business date and merchant partition in parallel.

16
00:01:34,669 --> 00:01:39,248
And here is the proof. The engine's findings are compared with the ground truth.

17
00:01:39,288 --> 00:01:48,082
Every injected fault was found with the expected class, and no clean payment was flagged: one hundred percent precision and recall, for every fault type.

18
00:01:50,205 --> 00:01:55,400
The dashboard leads with money at risk: the total amount still unresolved in the ops queue.

19
00:01:55,440 --> 00:02:01,209
Every amount is stored as integer paise, never floating point, and shown in Indian number formatting.

20
00:02:01,249 --> 00:02:04,028
Items the engine fixed by itself are not counted.

21
00:02:04,519 --> 00:02:09,546
Next to it is the fourteen-day trend by business date, and the breakdown by exception type.

22
00:02:09,586 --> 00:02:12,275
Clicking a type opens the queue already filtered.

23
00:02:12,767 --> 00:02:24,980
Below, the aging buckets show how long money has been at risk, and this panel shows what the engine handled by itself: deduplicated callbacks, settlements that arrived within grace, and small fee adjustments.

24
00:02:26,724 --> 00:02:30,711
The ops queue lists every exception, largest amount at risk first.

25
00:02:33,202 --> 00:02:39,072
Filters narrow it down. Here, amount mismatches of at least a hundred rupees, oldest first.

26
00:02:39,112 --> 00:02:43,554
Pagination happens on the server, so this scales to millions of rows.

27
00:02:45,448 --> 00:02:48,483
Each exception opens with the three records side by side.

28
00:02:48,523 --> 00:02:57,979
This one is missing in the ledger: the gateway settled it and the bank credited it, but the merchant never received the callback, so its own books don't know the customer paid.

29
00:02:59,847 --> 00:03:08,391
Missing in gateway is the opposite: the merchant recorded the payment, but it never appeared in any settlement file, so no money was ever going to arrive.

30
00:03:10,275 --> 00:03:15,842
Missing in bank: the gateway says it settled, the grace window has expired, and nothing arrived.

31
00:03:15,882 --> 00:03:20,572
Asking the bank's SOAP service live confirms it has no credit for this reference.

32
00:03:22,453 --> 00:03:26,841
A duplicate bank credit: the same settlement reference was credited twice.

33
00:03:26,881 --> 00:03:29,654
The bank's SOAP service confirms the duplicate.

34
00:03:32,603 --> 00:03:39,828
A real amount mismatch: the ledger and the gateway disagree by more than the one-rupee tolerance, so a person has to look at it.

35
00:03:41,716 --> 00:03:47,245
An orphan credit: money arrived at the bank with a reference that matches nothing in the gateway or the ledger.

36
00:03:49,134 --> 00:03:50,779
Some cases close themselves.

37
00:03:50,819 --> 00:03:58,420
This settlement arrived after T plus two, but inside the five-day grace window, so a rule resolved it and wrote an audit entry.

38
00:04:00,310 --> 00:04:03,924
And here the gateway sent the same webhook several times.

39
00:04:03,964 --> 00:04:10,205
The dedupe table, keyed by event id and Kafka position, absorbed the copies, with no money effect.

40
00:04:15,055 --> 00:04:16,382
Now the analyst's view.

41
00:04:16,422 --> 00:04:22,442
Analysts work the queue, but they can't run reconciliation, read the audit log or approve adjustments.

42
00:04:22,482 --> 00:04:26,423
The run button isn't even shown, and the API would refuse the call.

43
00:04:26,964 --> 00:04:31,297
Small fee or rounding differences become adjustment journal entries.

44
00:04:31,337 --> 00:04:37,921
Up to fifty paise they post automatically. This one is above fifty paise, so it waits for a supervisor.

45
00:04:38,460 --> 00:04:44,767
Here the analyst works a missing-in-ledger case: assigns it, adds a comment, and resolves it with a reason.

46
00:04:51,460 --> 00:04:55,894
Every step lands in the audit log, which a database trigger keeps append-only.

47
00:04:55,934 --> 00:04:59,393
Re-running reconciliation will never overwrite this decision.

48
00:05:05,191 --> 00:05:08,931
Signed in as the supervisor, the adjustment can now be approved.

49
00:05:08,971 --> 00:05:12,120
That posts the journal entry and closes the exception.

50
00:05:12,671 --> 00:05:16,004
Now let's re-run reconciliation over the same dates.

51
00:05:16,044 --> 00:05:22,297
Nothing changes: the same result digest, no new exceptions, and the resolved cases stay resolved.

52
00:05:27,953 --> 00:05:29,175
Finally, a live day.

53
00:05:29,215 --> 00:05:35,738
This time the bank statements stop today, so credits for the newest payments simply haven't arrived yet.

54
00:05:35,778 --> 00:05:39,524
They are due by T plus two, and tolerated until T plus five.

55
00:05:41,957 --> 00:05:47,370
The dashboard keeps them separate, as expected in transit. They don't count as money at risk yet.

56
00:05:47,410 --> 00:05:52,204
They're re-checked on every run, and they escalate to the queue if the window expires.

57
00:05:52,750 --> 00:05:57,456
In the queue they show as pending expected, and they leave on their own when the credit arrives.

58
00:05:58,153 --> 00:06:11,821
Everything you saw is also available through the REST API, documented with OpenAPI: login, runs, exceptions with filters, case actions, adjustments, the dashboard, the audit log and the simulator.

59
00:06:12,349 --> 00:06:15,154
And this is the bank simulator's SOAP contract.

60
00:06:15,194 --> 00:06:20,723
Its get settlement status operation is what the exception page calls, through a JAXB client.

61
00:06:21,217 --> 00:06:22,554
All of this is measured.

62
00:06:22,594 --> 00:06:34,139
At fourteen days of one hundred thousand payments a day, the engine reconciles one point four million entities in under seventeen seconds at parallelism eight, with identical results at any parallelism.

63
00:06:34,179 --> 00:06:39,129
Seventy-nine backend tests and thirty-eight frontend tests run green in GitHub Actions.

64
00:06:39,169 --> 00:06:43,660
The code, the benchmarks, and the commands to reproduce them are all on GitHub.

